1. Bc4 {The bishop, at his queen's bishop's fourth square.} Kg6 {The king, at his knight's third
square.} 2. Kf4 {The king, at his bishop's fourth square.} Kf6 {The king, at his bishop's third
square.} 3. Nc3 {The knight, at his queen's bishop's third square.} Kg6 {The king, at his
knight's third square.} 4. Ne4 {The knight, at his king's fourth square. (b) NOTE: The knight
must endeavour to take away from the adverse king the opposite squares to the bishop's colour.}
Kh6 {The king, at his rook's third square.} 5. Kf5 {The king, at the adverse king's bishop's
fourth square.} Kh7 {The king, at his rook's second square. (c) NOTE: If he had played his king
to his rook's fourth square, you must have given check with the bishop at your king's second
square; and then, if he gets his king on the side of the corner of your bishop's colour, you
will mate him in a few moves.} 6. Kf6 {The king, at the adverse king's bishop's third square.}
Kh8 {The king, at his rook's square. (d) NOTE: If he had played his king to his rook's third
square, you must have played your bishop to your king's second square, to oblige his king to get
into the black corner, where your knight is very soon to force him out.} 7. Nd6 {The knight, at
the adverse queen's third square.} Kh7 {The king, at his rook's second square.} 8. Nf7 {The
knight, at the adverse king's bishop's second square. (e) NOTE: In this position, the mate is
forced in about eighteen or twenty moves,} Kg8 {The king, at his knight's square. (f) NOTE: Your
knight prevents his king from coming back into the corner. Now, the business of your knight must
be, to cut his king off from the black squares, while your bishop will keep him from the white
ones.} 9. Bd3 {The bishop, at his queen's third square.} Kf8 {The king, at his bishop's square.}
10. Bh7 {The bishop, at the adverse king's rook second square.} Ke8 {The king, at his home.} 11.
Ne5 {The knight, at the adverse king's fourth square.} Kf8 {The king, at his bishop's square.
(g) NOTE: As he might have played his king to his queen's square, I make a back-game of it. Riv}
